#!/bin/sh
# Generated docs must match the sources they are derived from.
#
# INSTALL, once per clone. Hooks are not tracked by git and cannot be
# auto-installed, so a fresh clone has no doc check until you run:
#
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# That is a local setting, not a committed one. Everyone who clones has to run
# it, and nothing reminds them. If this check has to be enforced rather than
# offered, it belongs in CI, which runs for every clone and every push with no
# setup step.
#
# Runs --check, which is a CONTENT comparison. That is the whole point: a plain
# generator run skips any doc whose mtime is newer than its inputs, and a doc
# that was committed after its source changed looks fresh by mtime even when
# its content is stale. testing/probe_map_chest.md was in exactly that state --
# a plain run reported "183 up to date" and wrote nothing while the committed
# doc was missing a provenance line that pickup_scan.py had carried since
# 3b69f06. Only --check caught it.
#
# This hook is unconditional because --check is fast enough to always run: it
# took 2m50s until neighbours() stopped re-reading every production file per
# script, and about 2 seconds after - the AST pass over 200 scripts is most of
# what it costs now. Do not add a skip-if-unrelated-files gate on the theory that
# this is slow; it is not, and a gate is a way for drift through.
#
# WHAT IT CHECKS is what GIT KNOWS ABOUT. --check used to walk testing/*.py on
# disk, which made it a global lock with no owner: on a machine where several
# people work at once, any agent with a half-written script on disk failed every
# other agent's commit with "MISSING <stem>.md" until somebody generated a doc
# for a file that was not supposed to be committed yet. `git ls-files` decides
# the set instead, and STAGED counts as tracked - so `git add script.py` without
# its .md still fails here, which is the case this hook exists for. If you want
# a doc rendered for an untracked script, say so with --all.
#
# Do not "fix" this to run the plain generator. That reintroduces the bug it
# exists to catch.

if ! command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "pre-commit: python3 not found, skipping doc drift check" >&2
    exit 0
fi

if [ ! -f testing/make_doc.py ]; then
    exit 0
fi

out=$(python3 testing/make_doc.py --check 2>&1)
status=$?

if [ $status -eq 0 ]; then
    exit 0
fi

echo "pre-commit: generated docs are out of date" >&2
echo "$out" >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  The .md files under testing/ are generated from the scripts themselves.
  Regenerate with:

      python3 testing/make_doc.py --force

  Use --force, not a plain run. A plain run skips any doc whose mtime is newer
  than its inputs, which is exactly the case where the committed content is
  stale. If --check still fails after --force, the drift is real: something
  changed in a way the generator does not capture, and the generator needs
  updating.

  To commit without the check, re-run with --no-verify and say so in the
  commit body. Do not do it quietly.

MSG

exit 1
